{"run_id": "r-adcd47b31ca1-1", "task_id": "retry-q2-t0384-t0001", "trace_id": "retry-q2-t0384-trace0001", "member_id": "s1-r1-m11", "planned_activity_id": "retry-q2-t0384-p0001", "phase_id": "phase1", "workday": 1, "simulation_time": "2025-05-05T13:00:00.009000+08:00", "workflow_id": "software_feature", "call_id": "tool-02967eb8d16a488abd0c33054b2cb32b", "from_state": "issue_created", "tool_name": "design_document", "event_type": "tool_call", "to_state": "designed", "arguments": {"content": "Design document for compute(a, b) feature.\n\n## Overview\nImplement a public function `compute(a, b)` in `src/main.py` that returns the maximum of two finite numeric inputs.\n\n## Requirements\n- `compute(2, 3)` must return `3`.\n- Support zero, negative, and fractional values (e.g., `compute(-1, 0)` -> `0`, `compute(2.5, 2.4)` -> `2.5`, `compute(-3.5, -2.5)` -> `-2.5`).\n- Inputs must be finite numeric values (int or float, including bool as int subclass is acceptable but not required to special-case).\n- Non-numeric or non-finite inputs (NaN, infinity, strings, None) are outside the supported domain; behavior for them is unspecified but a defensive TypeError/ValueError may be raised.\n\n## Design\n- Location: `src/main.py`, module-level function `compute(a, b)`.\n- Implementation: validate that both arguments are real numbers (`numbers.Real`) and finite (`math.isfinite`), then return the larger of the two using a simple comparison (`b if b > a else a`) to avoid relying on built-in `max` semantics and to preserve the original numeric type of the winning argument.\n- No external dependencies; standard library only.\n\n## Validation\n- Public behavioral check: `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python checks/public_check.py`.\n- Build: `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python -m compileall -q src`.\n\n## Risks\n- None significant; pure function with no side effects.", "input_artifact_id": "fe3d10685f93"}, "result": {"tool_name": "design_document", "message": "design document created", "output_artifact_ids": ["0146fe83b262"], "output_paths": ["artifacts/0146fe83b262.md"], "evidence_level": "simulation_only", "business_status": "not_measured", "payload": "Design document for compute(a, b) feature.\n\n## Overview\nImplement a public function `compute(a, b)` in `src/main.py` that returns the maximum of two finite numeric inputs.\n\n## Requirements\n- `compute(2, 3)` must return `3`.\n- Support zero, negative, and fractional values (e.g., `compute(-1, 0)` -> `0`, `compute(2.5, 2.4)` -> `2.5`, `compute(-3.5, -2.5)` -> `-2.5`).\n- Inputs must be finite numeric values (int or float, including bool as int subclass is acceptable but not required to special-case).\n- Non-numeric or non-finite inputs (NaN, infinity, strings, None) are outside the supported domain; behavior for them is unspecified but a defensive TypeError/ValueError may be raised.\n\n## Design\n- Location: `src/main.py`, module-level function `compute(a, b)`.\n- Implementation: validate that both arguments are real numbers (`numbers.Real`) and finite (`math.isfinite`), then return the larger of the two using a simple comparison (`b if b > a else a`) to avoid relying on built-in `max` semantics and to preserve the original numeric type of the winning argument.\n- No external dependencies; standard library only.\n\n## Validation\n- Public behavioral check: `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python checks/public_check.py`.\n- Build: `/opt/anaconda3/bin/python -m compileall -q src`.\n\n## Risks\n- None significant; pure function with no side effects.", "output_paths_kind": "read_only_capture_evidence", "business_path": null, "workspace": "/Users/ayang/Desktop/project_work/Chimera/docs/dataset-evaluation/reports/stage2/full-from-scratch-20261015/q2-q3/failure-rerun-20261016/tasks/q2-t0384/study/workspaces/retry-q2-t0384-t0001", "execution_environment": {"cwd": "/Users/ayang/Desktop/project_work/Chimera/docs/dataset-evaluation/reports/stage2/full-from-scratch-20261015/q2-q3/failure-rerun-20261016/tasks/q2-t0384/study/workspaces/retry-q2-t0384-t0001", "python_executable": "/opt/anaconda3/bin/python", "default_build_command": "/opt/anaconda3/bin/python -m compileall -q src", "build_kind": "python_bytecode_compilation", "optional_modules": {"pytest": true, "build": false, "pip": true, "setuptools": true}}}, "status": "success", "schema_version": "chimera-event/1", "event_seq": 9, "event_id": "0fa33ee0188c48b5923dbf6313bbb342", "wall_time_utc": "2026-10-16T07:51:55.844618+00:00"}
